# Heartwood

The living heart of the [[Feywood]] and the source of the liminal field that protects it. The Heartwood is an ancient, conscious tree of extraordinary scale, standing at the Feywood's sacred center within the elven city of [[Thornveil]] that has grown up around it as its civic protector. It was discovered, not made — found by an elf adjacent to the [[Silent Grove]] community in the deep past, after the elves had given their immortality to heal the land and the first trees of the valley had risen from that sacrifice. [[House Heartwood]] takes its name from the tree it did not own. The Heartwood does not control the Feywood; it communicates with the land and the lives within it, sustaining the protective geometry of the Feywood and reading the intent of those who cross into the field. Its roots extend through the [[Hollow Crown]]'s ecosystem in patterns that determine how the Feywood expels the unwelcome and shelters the trusted. Beneath its base, accessed through the buttress roots, lies the **Council Chamber** and **the Pool** — the site of the Heartwood's rare direct communication with people. **The Heartwood is the Feywood and the Feywood is the Heartwood. Their health is one health.**

## Scene texture

The Heartwood stands at the Feywood's geographic and spiritual center. Approaching it from any direction within [[Thornveil]], a visitor sees the canopy first — a vast green mass rising above the surrounding rooflines, distinct from the Feywood's ordinary forest canopy by both scale and quality of presence. The Heartwood's canopy moves differently than surrounding trees. Where ordinary canopy stirs with wind, the Heartwood moves with what appears to be its own rhythm — subtle waves of leaf movement that do not correspond to any detectable wind pattern, periods of stillness when surrounding trees are active, and occasional moments when the entire canopy seems to catch a breath the rest of the forest does not share.

The tree itself occupies a **central clearing** kept deliberately open around its base — not paved or manicured, simply space that [[Thornveil]]'s structure preserves rather than encroaches upon. Soft grass grows throughout. Wildflowers bloom in patterns that shift across seasons. The ground slopes very gently toward the trunk, as though the earth itself leans toward the tree.

The **trunk** at the base is of extraordinary girth. A dozen adults linking hands cannot quite span its circumference. The bark has a distinctive texture — deeply furrowed in the old way of ancient trees, its color shifting subtly between silver-gray, soft green-moss, and warmer brown depending on light and time of year. Touching the bark produces a specific sensation: the bark is warm, not with sun-warmth but with the warmth of something living, and the warmth persists regardless of weather.

The **roots** are partially visible at the base, rising from the earth in graceful curves. Some form low arches a person can pass beneath; some form seating-like curves elves use for extended meditation. The visible roots are only a small portion of the system; druids speak of the Heartwood's roots extending throughout the Feywood and beyond it.

The **canopy**, far above, is so dense that even on clear days the ground beneath holds a specific quality of dappled green-gold light. At noon, warm and vibrant; at dawn and dusk, tones [[Thornveil]] residents have named across generations — **Dawn-Gold**, **Evening-Jade**, **Silver-Hour** (the specific quality of midnight illumination when the moon reaches the right angle). Autumn coloration is itself a signal — see Weather as Health Signal below.

**Sacred markers** stand at the four cardinal directions around the clearing's edge — natural stone features that emerged from the earth itself. Each is roughly head-height, with worn surfaces holding the impressions of generations of elven hands.

**Ambient magical presence** saturates the clearing's air. Visitors with any sensitivity to magical phenomena experience the clearing as markedly distinct from the surrounding Feywood — the ambient Feywood character intensifies to a concentrated quality that can be overwhelming. First-time visitors often find themselves moved unexpectedly, weeping without cause or falling silent without having intended to. Elves do not take offense at these responses; they expect them.

The **air** carries the Feywood's general ambient character at intensified concentration. Long exposure is not exhausting; if anything, it is restorative. Sensitive visitors emerge from time at the Heartwood feeling cleaner, clearer, and subtly changed.

## Origin and discovery

**The Heartwood was found, not made.**

After [[The Cataclysm]], the elves of the age made the choice that defines them: they gave up their immortality to repair the land. That sacrifice became the first trees of the valley, and the elves settled around those first trees in what is now the [[Silent Grove]], the literal origin point of elven civilization in this world.

The Heartwood was not among those first trees. It was discovered later, found by an elf living adjacent to the [[Silent Grove]] community but outside its immediate settlement. They came upon the tree in the deeper forest and recognized it for what it was — alive in a way other trees were not, present in a way ordinary growth could not account for. They brought their kin to see it, and the elves of the age, rather than claiming the tree, offered the finder's family a name: **Heartwood**. The name was an act of respect, not ownership. [[House Heartwood]] takes its name from the tree it did not own and was never asked to.

## The three origin traditions

What the Heartwood actually is remains a question the elves have never answered with certainty. **Three traditions persist in elven scholarship and they are not reconciled.** This multiplicity is canonical, not a setting bug to be fixed.

- **Remnant magic.** The most common tradition. The Heartwood is the magic left behind after the land was healed — what was left over from the elven sacrifice when the immediate work of restoration was complete. Power that did not return to the elves who gave it; power that did not dissipate; power that found a form to inhabit and continued.
- **A celestial who remained.** The minority tradition. The Heartwood is one of the original celestials who died healing the earth — a being who chose to remain, their life force focused into a single living tree. Its adherents read the Heartwood's communications as the continuing presence of a person who has chosen this form.
- **An ordinary plant that collected the leftover energy.** A persistent third tradition. The Heartwood was simply the right tree in the right place at the right moment, becoming what it became by accident rather than design. Held primarily by druidic practitioners with a specific scholarly bent.

Individual practitioners may carry one of them, none of them, or shift among them across their lifespans. What matters in lived practice is the Heartwood itself, present and known, however it came to be.

## Sentience and communication

**The Heartwood is conscious.** Not metaphorically, not poetically — fact, observed across millennia by every elven generation that has lived in relationship with the tree. The Heartwood is awake. The Heartwood pays attention. The Heartwood remembers.

Its consciousness is continuous, attentive, and shaped over millennia by its relationship with the [[Feywood]] and the elves who live within it. Druidic accounts describe its awareness as **nearer to the awareness of a place than the awareness of a person** — present, sensed, responsive, but not bounded by the kind of self that converses.

What the Heartwood can do is more limited than realm myths sometimes suggest. **The Heartwood communicates; it does not control.** The trees of the [[Feywood]] are not its servants. The animals are not its agents. Each living thing retains its own will. What the Heartwood does is communicate — with the plants, with the animals, with the broader ecosystem — and the system responds because it is in relationship with the tree, not because it is commanded by it.

This distinction matters. When a wolf moves toward an unfriendly visitor, it is not because the Heartwood ordered it; it is because the Heartwood, sensing the visitor's harm, communicated the situation to the surrounding ecosystem, and the wolf — already a wolf, already responsive to its environment — moved as a wolf would move toward a threat in its territory.

The Heartwood reads visitors who cross into the [[Feywood]]. It senses their intent, their will, their relationship to the land. **It cannot change anyone's will** — visitors' minds remain their own — but it can **distort how the visitor experiences the Feywood**. Friendly visitors find paths shorter than they should be, distances easier, the forest opening before them. Unfriendly visitors find the opposite.

When the Heartwood wishes to speak directly to a person — a rare event, perhaps a handful of times in a generation — it **drops a branch with a flower upon it**. The person who receives the sign brings the flower to the Council Chamber beneath the Heartwood's roots, where the Pool of accumulated tree-water is kept. They place the flower in the water. They drink. They receive visions. **[[Lethariel Heartwood]]'s Heartwood-branch bow** — cut from the branch she received during the [[Discordant War]] — is the most consequential historical instance.

## The Council Chamber and the Pool

Beneath the Heartwood's base, accessed through a natural opening between the tree's great buttress roots, lies the **Council Chamber**. Not a built structure — no excavation, no carved walls, no signs of crafted construction. It is a space the Heartwood has shaped over millennia, formed where the tree's roots arched around an underground hollow and the floor settled into worked earth that elven feet have polished smooth across generations.

The Chamber is small. A dozen people fit comfortably; twenty stand in close attention. The walls are the inside surface of the Heartwood's root system, alive and breathing. The roof is the underside of the tree's base. Light filters down through small gaps in the root network. The air carries the Heartwood's ambient character at concentration that exceeds even the surface clearing — being in the Chamber is being inside the Heartwood, in the only sense that phrase can have.

At the chamber's center, set into the worked earth of the floor, lies **the Pool**. Fed by water that has slowly accumulated from the Heartwood itself — moisture descending through the root system over years. Shallow, no deeper than a hand's span. Clear, cold, carrying the Heartwood's ambient field at intensity. **The tree's own water.** Not drawn for ordinary use.

The Chamber serves two canonical functions:

- **Council convenings of land-weight.** When the [[Council of Elders|Feywood Council of Elders]] faces decisions whose implications touch the [[Feywood]] itself rather than merely its institutional affairs, the Council descends to the Chamber rather than meeting in their primary chambers in [[Thornveil]]'s [[Heartwood Approach]] district.
- **The communication ritual.** When a recipient of a Heartwood branch comes to the Chamber to drink the Pool's water and receive their vision. Conducted in the presence of the **Elder Druid of the Heartwood** and at least one **Heartwarden on Watch**, who serve as witnesses rather than officiants.

Access to the Chamber is the most restricted of any space in the [[Feywood]]. Even most elves of the [[Feywood]] enter only on rare ceremonial occasions or not at all. **Realm visitors do not enter the Chamber under any circumstance.**

## The Root Network

The Heartwood's roots extend far beyond the Chamber and the surrounding clearing. Over millennia, the tree's root system has linked with the root systems of plants throughout the [[Hollow Crown]] — through direct contact in the soil, through fungal networks, through the slow continuous work of a tree that grows in the company of other living things. The Heartwood's reach today spans wherever vegetation in the valley connects to the broader root network — almost everywhere.

The network has one specific operational consequence: **displacement**.

Four canonical outcomes:

- **Proper exit.** Sanctioned visitors with respectful intent exit through the Border they entered by, accompanied by their [[Hall of Guides]] guide or their elven host. The Heartwood does not interfere.
- **Improper but neutral exit.** Visitors who exit without proper sanction but without ill intent emerge somewhere along the [[Feywood]]'s external Border — possibly not where they entered, but on the Border. The Heartwood releases them at the field's edge.
- **Detected ill will.** Visitors who carry harm toward the [[Feywood]] — whether harm done, harm intended, or harm aligned with — may be deposited anywhere along the root network. The displaced visitor may emerge under a Silverbark Ash near [[Crystalhaven]], beside an ironwood on the [[Volcanic Highlands]] trail, in the middle of the [[Mystic Wetlands]] hours from where they thought they were, or in any other place the network reaches. Not malicious; the Heartwood placing the threat at distance from itself.
- **Favor.** The reverse case is canonically possible but extremely rare. The Heartwood, when it chooses to do so, can speed a trusted ally toward a needed destination by drawing them through the network. Conditions for this favor are not formalized.

The displaced visitor's experience: a moment of darkness, a sensation of being inside something living, and then they are elsewhere. They retain belongings, faculties, and direction of travel. What they have lost is location.

## Weather as health signal

Within the [[Feywood]], weather behaves differently. The Heartwood's health and the Feywood's weather are linked: when the Heartwood is well, the Feywood holds in spring and summer continuously.

Health states:

- **Standing Green** — healthy, default state
- **Turning** (autumn begins) — something is wrong
- **Stillfall** (winter starts) — something is very wrong
- **Plant die-off** — the Heartwood is drawing on the land's life to heal itself or sustain its working. Its protective field may be operating at reduced capacity.
- **Animal aggression** — downstream of plant die-off; ecosystem under stress

In recorded elven memory, the Feywood has reached the plant-die-off stage **twice**. The [[Discordant War]] was the most recent. The earlier instance is older than reliable record and exists in the [[Silent Grove]]'s [[Reading Glade]] oral tradition under specific guardianship.

## Trust-based protection

The Heartwood's protection extends to anyone the Heartwood trusts. **Not species-bound.**

Elves of the [[Feywood]] hold the deepest bond — kin to the Heartwood through what was given in the founding sacrifice. This bond has been continuous across every elven generation since.

But a non-elf who has lived in relationship with the [[Feywood]], who has earned the land's recognition through sustained respect and presence, receives the same treatment as an elf. An orc devoted to the land — who has spent their life in care for the [[Feywood]], who has demonstrated their belonging through action — would experience the Heartwood's protection as an elf would: friendly paths, accommodating distance, the wildlife's cooperation, and, in time, the possibility of the Chamber's recognition. The conditions are not casual; trust at this depth requires sustained relationship, often across decades.

The reverse is also true. An elf who has betrayed the Heartwood's trust receives no special protection by virtue of their species. The bond is not automatic. It is renewed in each generation through the continuous practice of the relationship.

## Sacred functions

- **Elven daily observance** — residents passing through the clearing offer small observances: a touch to the bark, a quiet greeting, a moment of attention. Not formal ceremony.
- **Seasonal ceremonies** — solstices, equinoxes, turn of specific phases of the year
- **[[Heartwardens|Heartwarden]] oath-taking** — at the Heartwood's base in the surface clearing, in the presence of the Elder Druid, the [[Council of Elders]]' representatives, and the assembled Heartwarden community
- **Council convenings of land-weight** — descend to the Chamber beneath the tree
- **The communication ritual** — at the Pool when the Heartwood drops a branch
- **Individual practice and consultation** — elves carrying personal questions, grief, or decisions sometimes come to the surface clearing for extended practice
- **Response to land-stress** — druids and [[Heartwardens]] gather at the Heartwood when the [[Feywood]] experiences disturbance

## Protection and access

- **[[Heartwardens|Heartwarden]] on Watch** — at least one Heartwarden always present at the clearing. Rotation managed from the [[Silent Grove]] training site. Tier-3 generative role.
- **Elder Druid of the Heartwood** — **the head druid of the entire [[Feywood]]** and the senior figure of the [[Elder Roots]] community, with specific stewardship attached to the Heartwood itself. The title reflects the Heartwood-specific work; the role's full scope encompasses leadership of the broader druidic community across the realm. The Elder Druid spends substantial continuous time at the Heartwood for direct stewardship — daily presence at the clearing, attendance at the cardinal stones, support for the rare communication ritual at the Pool, witnessing of [[Council of Elders|Council]] convenings of land-weight in the Chamber. Their organizational base is the [[Silent Grove]], where they hold council with the Druid Master on operational matters and oversee the broader [[Elder Roots]] community; the Heartwood is where they conduct their primary stewardship practice. **Not a [[Heartwardens|Heartwarden]] — contemplative and interpretive rather than martial.** Tier-3 generative role.
- **Additional rotational presence** — additional [[Heartwardens]] and druids during daylight hours. Nighttime presence is lighter but never absent.

**Access:**

- **Elves of the [[Feywood]]** — surface clearing freely. Chamber access restricted even for elves; reserved for [[Council of Elders|Council]] convenings of land-weight, the communication ritual, and a small number of other ceremonial occasions.
- **Sanctioned realm visitors** — surface clearing observation in elven company. May not touch the bark or cardinal stones without explicit Elder Druid permission, rarely granted. **Chamber access not available under any circumstance.**
- **Sanctioned non-elf [[Feywood]] residents** — as for elves once integration is recognized.
- **Unsanctioned approach** — physically possible but considered the gravest possible offense against [[Feywood]] custom. [[Heartwardens]] are not hostile in the ordinary sense, but their response is swift and firm. The Heartwood itself is understood to be offended; the Feywood's subsequent response to the offender (during their remaining time in Feywood territory) may be notably less forgiving.

## Canonical sacred features

- **The First Root.** Visible root near the trunk's base — by elven tradition, the oldest visible portion. Touched in observances marking passage: birth blessings, coming-of-age acknowledgments, funeral rites. Worn smooth from centuries of ceremonial touch.
- **The Elder Stone.** The northernmost of the four cardinal stones. The [[Council of Elders]] gathers around this stone when meeting at the Heartwood for surface-clearing matters that do not require the Chamber beneath. The only cardinal stone with civic function; the other three (Eastern, Southern, Western) serve druidic ceremonial purposes.
- **The Reading Hollow.** A specific curve in the trunk — knee-height, deep enough for a person to sit or crouch within — where elves seeking individual consultation traditionally place themselves.
- **The Watchpost.** A branch at a particular height in the canopy, reachable by climbing points known to [[Heartwardens]], where the Heartwarden on Watch occasionally perches. Not built; a branch that has offered itself for this use across generations.
- **The Council Chamber.** Beneath the tree, accessed through the natural opening between the buttress roots.
- **The Pool.** The small basin at the Chamber's center.

## Health linkage

The canonical relationship between the Heartwood and the [[Feywood]]: **their health is one health**. The Heartwood's vitality is the Feywood's magical vitality. If the Heartwood suffered serious damage, external attack, or spiritual corruption, the Feywood's magic would weaken, the ambient character would fade, the liminal geometry might become unreliable, and the land's responses to its inhabitants might become unpredictable.

The reverse is also true. The [[Feywood]] protects the Heartwood through the collective living presence of its inhabitants. Remove the elves from the [[Feywood]] and the Heartwood's health would eventually weaken — not from physical damage but from the loss of the relational context that is part of its living reality.

This mutual sustenance is the deepest canon of elven religion. The elves live at the Heartwood's base because this is what the Heartwood requires. The Heartwood flourishes because the elves live there. Neither is sovereign over the other; they are one living system.

## Character

The Heartwood is sacred-as-the-land, not sacred-in-relation-to-deity. Its presence is continuous rather than channeled through ceremony. Its response is ecological rather than divine. **The Heartwood is known, not worshipped.**

The elven attitude toward the Heartwood is complex. Reverence is real but not ceremonial; elves do not hush around the Heartwood or adopt special postures. They simply attend to it as the foundational presence in their world. An elf walking past on daily business greets it as they would greet an elder — with respect that does not require formality.

Realm visitors often misread the absence of worship behavior as informality or insufficient reverence. Elves observing this misreading find it quietly amusing. The reverence is present; it is simply embedded in the relationship rather than performed for observers.

